<section xml:id="sec-mod3-assessment">
  <title>Module 3: Assessment</title>
  <p>Submitted Proofs and Reflection &#x2014; Estimated time: 40 minutes</p>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->

    <p>
      This assessment has three parts. Parts 1 and 2 ask you to write
      complete mathematical proofs, submitted for feedback. Part 3 is
      a short reflection. Submit all three parts together as a single
      document (PDF or typed file) through the course management system.
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Write-up standard.</alert> All proofs must be written in
      complete mathematical prose. Use the style modeled in the Worked
      Examples: include scratch work (clearly labeled) before the formal
      proof, announce your strategy, write in complete grammatical
      sentences, and justify every step. Work consisting only of symbolic
      manipulations without explanation will not receive full credit.
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>A note on Part 2.</alert> All students complete all three
      parts. For Part 2, choose the option that best matches your
      background: if you have prior real analysis experience, challenge
      yourself with Option B. If this is newer territory, Option A is
      the right starting point.
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Academic integrity.</alert> Your proofs must be your own
      work. You may refer to your notes, the textbooks, and the worked
      examples from this module. You may not collaborate with other
      students on the written proofs or use AI writing tools to draft
      your arguments.
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Part 1: Core Proof (Required)</alert>
      (Estimated time: 15 minutes)
    </p>
    <p>
      The following set equality is one of De Morgan's laws for set
      difference. It is structurally similar to Worked Example 1,
      but involves a different operation and requires a different
      case analysis.
    </p>

  <!-- ============================================================
       PART 1
  ============================================================ -->

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod3-as-1">
    <title>De Morgan's Law for Set Difference</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, and <m>C</m> be sets. Prove:
        <md>A \setminus (B \cap C) = (A \setminus B) \cup (A \setminus C).</md>
        Your proof must use the element-chasing method: show each
        set is a subset of the other. Write the proof in two
        clearly labeled directions.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        Recall that <m>x \in A \setminus (B \cap C)</m> means
        <m>x \in A</m> and <m>x \notin B \cap C</m>.
        The statement <m>x \notin B \cap C</m> means it is not the
        case that (<m>x \in B</m> and <m>x \in C</m>). By De Morgan's
        law for logic, this is equivalent to:
        <m>x \notin B</m> or <m>x \notin C</m>.
        Now consider the two cases.
      </p>
    </hint>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================
       PART 2
  ============================================================ -->

    <p>
      <alert>Part 2: Proof Choice (Required &#x2014; Choose One)</alert>
      (Estimated time: 20 minutes)
    </p>
    <p>
      Choose <em>one</em> of the following two proof problems.
      Both ask for a complete proof; they differ in difficulty and
      the techniques required. Label your submission clearly with
      <q>Option A</q> or <q>Option B.</q>
    </p>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod3-as-2a">
    <title>Option A (Standard): Multiples of 3 Are Countably Infinite</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>T = \{3n : n \in \Z\}</m> be the set of all integer
        multiples of 3.
        <ol marker="(i)">
          <li>
            Construct an explicit bijection <m>f : \N \to T</m>.
            (Use the bijection <m>\varphi : \N \to \Z</m> from
            <xref ref="ex-mod3-we-4" text="custom">Worked Example 4</xref>
            as a guide, or construct a direct bijection by interleaving
            positive and negative multiples of 3.)
          </li>
          <li>
            Prove that your function is injective.
          </li>
          <li>
            Prove that your function is surjective.
          </li>
          <li>
            Conclude that <m>T</m> is countably infinite.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        One approach: define <m>f : \N \to T</m> by
        <m>f(n) = 3 \cdot \varphi(n)</m>, where <m>\varphi : \N \to \Z</m>
        is the bijection from Worked Example 4. Then verify that
        <m>f</m> is a bijection <m>\N \to T</m>.
      </p>
    </hint>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod3-as-2b">
    <title>Option B (More Challenging): Countable Union of Countable Sets</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> be countable sets. Prove that
        <m>A \cup B</m> is countable.
      </p>
      <p>
        You may use the following facts without proof:
        <m>\N \times \{0,1\}</m> is countably infinite (since it is
        in bijection with <m>\N</m>), and any subset of a countable
        set is finite or countably infinite (Zorn Proposition 1.17).
        You may not invoke Proposition 1.19 (Zorn) directly, as that
        is the general version of the result you are proving.
      </p>
      <p>
        Your proof should:
        <ol marker="(i)">
          <li>
            Handle the case where at least one of <m>A</m> or <m>B</m>
            is finite separately, or explain why it suffices to assume
            both are countably infinite.
          </li>
          <li>
            Construct an explicit surjection from <m>\N</m> onto
            <m>A \cup B</m>, or an explicit injection from <m>A \cup B</m>
            into a known countable set.
          </li>
          <li>
            Use Proposition 1.17 to conclude.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        If <m>A = \emptyset</m> or <m>B = \emptyset</m>, then
        <m>A \cup B</m> is <m>B</m> or <m>A</m>, which is countable.
        Otherwise <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are nonempty and countable, so
        there exist surjections <m>a : \N \to A</m> and
        <m>b : \N \to B</m> (a bijection if the set is countably
        infinite; if <m>A = \{x_1, \ldots, x_k\}</m> is finite, take
        <m>a(n) = x_{\min(n,k)}</m>). Define <m>h : \N \to A \cup B</m> by
        sending even <m>n</m> to <m>a(n/2)</m> and odd <m>n</m>
        to <m>b((n+1)/2)</m>. Show <m>h</m> is a surjection. Then turn
        it into an injection: for each <m>y \in A \cup B</m>, let
        <m>\iota(y)</m> be the least <m>n \in \N</m> with
        <m>h(n) = y</m> (every nonempty set of natural numbers has a
        least element). If <m>\iota(y) = \iota(y')</m>, then
        <m>y = h(\iota(y)) = h(\iota(y')) = y'</m>, so
        <m>\iota : A \cup B \to \N</m> is injective. Thus
        <m>A \cup B</m> is in bijection with the subset
        <m>\iota(A \cup B)</m> of <m>\N</m>, and Proposition 1.17
        finishes the proof.
      </p>
    </hint>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================
       PART 3: REFLECTION
  ============================================================ -->

    <p>
      <alert>Part 3: Reflection</alert>
      (Estimated time: 5 minutes)
    </p>
    <p>
      Write 3&#x2013;5 sentences for each question. These are graded
      for thoughtfulness, not for mathematical correctness.
    </p>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod3-as-r1">
    <title>Bauldry &#xa7;2.1 in Set-Theoretic Language</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Identify one place in Bauldry &#xa7;2.1 where a set-theoretic
        argument from Module 3 (membership, subset, union, intersection,
        set difference, or element-chasing) is used. Describe in
        2&#x2013;3 sentences what the argument is and why the set operation
        involved matters in that context.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod3-as-r2">
    <title>Element-Chasing as a Proof Template</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        In Part 1 and Worked Example 1, you used the element-chasing
        method. Describe the template in your own words: what is the
        general structure of an element-chasing proof of a set equality?
        Then reflect: was there a step in your Part 1 proof where you
        had to make a choice about how to proceed? What guided that
        choice?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod3-as-r3">
    <title>Looking Ahead</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        In Module 4, you will prove convergence of sequences using
        <m>\varepsilon</m>-<m>N</m> arguments. A sequence is a function
        <m>a : \N \to \R</m>. Based on what you learned in Module 3
        about functions, injectivity, and countability, describe one
        connection you see between the material of Module 3 and the
        idea of a convergent sequence. What properties of the function
        <m>a</m> (if any) are relevant to whether the sequence converges?
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================
       RUBRIC
  ============================================================ -->

    <p>
      <alert>Assessment Rubric (Informational)</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      The following rubric describes how submitted proofs will be evaluated.
    </p>

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod3-as-rubric">
    <title>Grading Criteria</title>
    <tabular halign="left">
      <col width="14%"/>
      <col width="29%"/>
      <col width="29%"/>
      <col width="28%"/>
      <row header="yes">
        <cell><p>Criterion</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Full credit</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Partial credit</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Minimal credit</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p><alert>Logical correctness</alert></p></cell>
        <cell><p>All steps valid; the argument proves exactly the stated claim</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Mostly correct with one fixable gap</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Significant logical errors or wrong claim proved</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p><alert>Use of definitions</alert></p></cell>
        <cell><p>Set operations and function properties invoked by name and unpacked from definition</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Definitions cited but not fully unpacked in at least one step</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Definitions not cited; argument proceeds by intuition only</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p><alert>Element-chasing structure (Part 1)</alert></p></cell>
        <cell><p>Both subset directions clearly labeled and complete; arbitrary element introduced correctly</p></cell>
        <cell><p>One direction complete; the other sketched or reversed</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Element-chasing structure missing; equality claimed without proof of both directions</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p><alert>Function construction (Part 2)</alert></p></cell>
        <cell><p>Explicit function of the kind the option asks for (a bijection in Option A; a surjection or injection in Option B) given; every property it needs verified</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Function described informally; only some of the needed properties verified</p></cell>
        <cell><p>No explicit function; conclusion asserted without construction</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p><alert>Mathematical prose</alert></p></cell>
        <cell><p>Complete grammatical sentences; scratch work labeled and separate from proof</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Mostly prose with some missing connective sentences</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Primarily symbolic with no connecting narrative</p></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><p><alert>Reflection</alert></p></cell>
        <cell><p>Specific, thoughtful, and engages with the mathematical content</p></cell>
        <cell><p>General but relevant observations</p></cell>
        <cell><p>Vague or too brief to be informative</p></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </paragraphs>

</section>
